[17592186162668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162668 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=48.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec22f795-26e2-5bc9-b423-bed373ca22ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162668 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=48\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ find the principal axes of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "611baa25-d6cc-5c6f-9064-69e0865e9f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162719 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162856 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162856 #edb/ref 1061 30544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162856 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3ecee7b-206f-5bb5-9257-61e0758f2151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162856 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162900 #edb/ref 1061 30924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51f3b5b0-1a5d-56cf-a16d-5c612010bfbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162901 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162901 #edb/ref 1061 30925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162901 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d0b9890-4620-5b89-b7da-8090ecf6f057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162901 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162923 #edb/ref 1061 31528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25baa4e8-cd30-578f-b8a2-6f5442b1bca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162938 #edb/ref 1061 31799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d3c6630-85aa-524d-9982-1c2a440c6b5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162949 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162949 #edb/ref 1061 32221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab944e55-c021-5455-a8c9-8144173eabb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163231 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y},$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix. \n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A,$ which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation \n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14,\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +14 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2.$ \n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to \n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163231 #edb/ref 1065 4890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163231 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2f9d7d81-cc50-5024-8f69-3b529968aeac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163231 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163256 #edb/ref 1064 "In some special cases, a quadratic equation might generate a degenerate curve or even an empty set.\n\n- For example, consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-3x_1^2+8x_1x_2+3x_2^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_1^2 - 5y_2^2 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich represents two intersecting lines $y_1 = y_2$ and $y_1 = -y_2.$\n\n- Now consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-2x_1^2+8x_1x_2-8x_2^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n- 10y_2^2 =10\n$$\n\nwhich represents an empty set as the equation $y_2^2 = -1$ does not have any real solution.\n\n- Finally, reducing the left-hand side of \n\n$$\nx_1^2-4x_1x_2+4x_2^2 = 5\n$$\n\nto the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_2^2 =5\n$$\n\nwhich represents a pair of parallel lines $y_2 = 1$ and $y_2=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163256 #edb/ref 1065 5077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163256 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cf07f443-3757-578e-908a-51eaef4cde16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163256 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Special Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1045 "Reducing a Quadratic Curve to Its Principal Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186411465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411429 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5c959c8a-bb4d-57d5-8ba1-a3ae664b0bd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411430 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411430 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c050d04-cc57-5e4f-9981-5ac992758bf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411431 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411431 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411431 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15485fac-790e-5582-90f5-2eb0336ce8